But all three professors also know the potential threats reach beyond their campuses. The internet has amplified so much of this behavior, making it easier for someone to become a stalker and easier for anyone to be stalked.

orFor Catherine Tan, this experience of being, in a sense, a collateral victim of a cyberstalker who started out obsessed with someone else went from irritating to infuriating. Sometimes, the racial taunting especially hits a nerve. “I’m Vietnamese. I was born in the US,” Tan told me. “Growing up in the ’90s and early 2000s, at that time, American culture wasn’t as welcoming.” Some of S.’s comments would claim Tan “wants to be white.” Tan told me she does not want to be white, but she did struggle to embrace her Asian American identity in her younger years. Dealing with such comments over and over, even in adulthood, was at times depleting. But it was S.’s letter to Vassar that crossed a line.","entities":["Catherine Tan","Vassar","The obsessive tormenter who made professors’ lives miserable","stalker","racial taunting","American culture","Asian American identity","S.","US","internet","The Verge"],"facts":["The obsessive tormenter who made professors’ lives miserable -MENTIONS-> Supreme Court","The obsessive tormenter who made professors’ lives miserable -MENTIONS-> Virginia","The obsessive tormenter who made professors’ lives miserable -MENTIONS-> pandemic","The obsessive tormenter who made professors’ lives miserable -MENTIONS-> journalism","The obsessive tormenter who made professors’ lives miserable -MENTIONS-> academia"]}

