Tan, who received a copy of the email, explained: “She wrote this long letter accusing me of sexually harassing her, forcing her to be a lesbian.” S. signed with her full name. A similar email also arrived at the Title IX office of George Mason University: “My name is [S.] and I’m not a student nor have I ever been one at George Mason. I’m writing to you today regarding one of your employees, Janani Umamaheswar of criminology, at the university and their sexually harassing behavior towards me online on twitter.” The letter continued: “She has called me a lesbian, and has recommended that I have sexual relationships with women.” So far, the Title IX offices, departments, and administrators at Tan, Umamaheswar, and Sinha’s schools have been responsive and understanding about their experiences with their harassment. But all three professors also know the potential threats reach beyond their campuses.","entities":["Title IX Office","The obsessive tormenter who made professors’ lives miserable","sexual harassment","Janani Umamaheswar","George Mason University","criminology","Sinha","Tan","S.","Twitter","The Verge"],"facts":["The obsessive tormenter who made professors’ lives miserable -MENTIONS-> pandemic","The obsessive tormenter who made professors’ lives miserable -MENTIONS-> Supreme Court","Title IX Office -PART_OF-> Arizona State University","The obsessive tormenter who made professors’ lives miserable -MENTIONS-> journalism","The obsessive tormenter who made professors’ lives miserable -MENTIONS-> Virginia"]}
