I have taught journalism in academia for over a decade and have watched threats to teachers grow worse over time. In 2015, I became concerned about a student who professed having a crush on me, despite knowing I am married, and who told a colleague about his sexual feelings toward me. I reported my concerns about the inappropriate comments to my school. His behavior escalated. He already had a criminal record, including charges of sexual violence, and he talked about killing people and writing a book about it. Frightened students reported his conduct as well. His reading responses also turned dark, discussing rape and describing ideas of criminal activity, along with a lack of empathy toward murder and disaster victims. My department, program directors, and our humanities dean supported and backed me when I raised worries.","entities":["journalism","academia","the student","The obsessive tormenter who made professors’ lives miserable","murder","Sinha","the narrator","a colleague","the school","the department","program directors","the humanities dean","sexual violence","criminal activity","disaster"],"facts":["the student -SAID-> Umamaheswar","European consumer groups band together to fight Meta’s self-serving ad-free sub — branding it ‘unfair’ and ‘illegal’ -MENTIONS-> journalism","How OpenAI's ChatGPT has changed the world in just a year -MENTIONS-> academia","How the conspiracy-fueled Epoch Times went mainstream and made millions -MENTIONS-> journalism","the student -SAID-> possible hacking of her personal laptop computer"]}
